          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Suppose that ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "y=f(x)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is a function whose graph has been drawn.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $\\mathcal U$ be shifting upwards $a$ units and $\\mathcal H$ be reflection in $y=0$. Write down the equations of the successive graphs obtained by applying $\\mathcal U$, then $\\mathcal H$, then $\\mathcal U$, then $\\mathcal H$, and prove that the final graph is the same as the first. Confirm the equations by applying these operations successively to a square book or piece of paper.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y-a=f(x)$, $-y-a=f(x)$, $-y=f(x)$, $y=f(x)$.\n\nThe final graph is the original graph.",
          "worked": "The successive graph equations are\n$$\\begin{align*}y-a&=f(x)\\\\-y-a&=f(x)\\\\-(y-a)-a&=f(x)\\\\-y&=f(x)\\\\y&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThe third and fourth displayed rows describe the same stage, after the second upward shift. The last reflection restores the original equation.\n\nFor a marked point on the square book or paper, the successive vertical coordinates are $y+a$, $-y-a$, $-y$, then $y$. The horizontal coordinate stays fixed, confirming that every point returns.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $\\mathcal R$ be shifting right by $a$ units. Write down the equations of the successive graphs obtained by applying $\\mathcal R$, then $\\mathcal H$, then $\\mathcal R$, then $\\mathcal H$, and describe the final graph. Confirm using the square book.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=f(x-a)$, $-y=f(x-a)$, $-y=f(x-2a)$, $y=f(x-2a)$.\n\nThe final graph is the original graph shifted right by $2a$ units.",
          "worked": "The successive equations are\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=f(x-a)\\\\-y&=f(x-a)\\\\-y&=f(x-2a)\\\\y&=f(x-2a).\\end{align*}$$\nThe two horizontal shifts add. The two reflections restore the vertical coordinate. A marked point on the square book moves successively to $(x+a,y)$, $(x+a,-y)$, $(x+2a,-y)$ and $(x+2a,y)$, confirming the final translation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $\\mathcal V$ be reflection in $x=0$ and $\\mathcal I$ be reflection in $y=x$. Write down the equations of the successive graphs obtained by applying $\\mathcal I$, then $\\mathcal V$, then $\\mathcal I$, then $\\mathcal H$, and show that the final graph is the same as the first. Confirm using the square book.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=f(y)$, $-x=f(y)$, $-y=f(x)$, $y=f(x)$.\n\nThe final graph is the original graph.",
          "worked": "Reflection in $y=x$ exchanges the coordinates. Reflection in an axis changes the sign of the perpendicular coordinate. The successive equations are\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=f(y)\\\\-x&=f(y)\\\\-y&=f(x)\\\\y&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTrack a marked point on the square book through $(y,x)$, $(-y,x)$, $(x,-y)$ and $(x,y)$. Every point returns to its original position.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Write down the equations of the successive graphs obtained by applying the combination $\\mathcal I$-followed-by-$\\mathcal V$ once, twice, $\\ldots$, until the original graph returns. Confirm using the square book.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-x=f(y)$, $-y=f(-x)$, $x=f(-y)$, $y=f(x)$.\n\nFour applications return the original graph.",
          "worked": "Each application sends a point $(x,y)$ to $(-y,x)$, a $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise rotation about the origin. After one, two, three and four applications, the equations are\n$$\\begin{align*}-x&=f(y)\\\\-y&=f(-x)\\\\x&=f(-y)\\\\y&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nMark a point on the square book and follow its positions $(-y,x)$, $(-x,-y)$, $(y,-x)$ and $(x,y)$. Four quarter-turns restore every point. A graph with rotational symmetry may coincide with the original at an earlier stage.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
